# Physics Sim Brainstorm Notes

## Brainstorm Session
- We’re building a macOS-only, Metal-first 3D particle sim focused on both performance and education.
- Architecture stays modular with three independent swappable module types: `PhysicsModule`, `VisualModule`, and `OptimizationModule`.
- Infrastructure stack is `Swift + Xcode + Metal + MSL + MetalKit`, with `SwiftUI` for the interactive control panel.
- Apple profiling tools (`GPU Frame Capture`, `Instruments`) are part of core infrastructure.
- Every optimization method gets an educational visualization mode with runtime controls and measurable benchmarks.
- Initial presets:
  - `M1.2` (M1 Pro, 32 GB, 20 GB sim budget)
  - `M1` (M1 base, 8 GB, 4 GB sim budget)
- Performance intent: beat the prior ~700k playable-particle reference while keeping `M1` reasonably demonstrable.

## Purpose
- Capture rough brainstorming and evolving architecture ideas before implementation.
- Keep this as a living scratchpad for simulation design and optimization experiments.

## Project intent (current)
- Build a 3D physics simulation that runs on the GPU.
- Target very high particle counts.
- Use Apple's unified CPU/GPU memory model for effects and performance opportunities.
- This is not a game project.

## Platform and tooling direction
- Avoid Unity and other game engines.
- Prefer native macOS stack for control and performance:
  - Swift host app
  - Metal compute shaders for simulation
  - MetalKit (`MTKView`) for interactive 3D rendering surface
  - Optional debug/profiling tooling (Xcode GPU capture, Instruments)

## Core performance problem
- Naive particle interactions are O(n^2) and do not scale.
- Need broad-phase/neighbor-search optimizations to reduce pair checks.

## Architecture requirement: swappable modules
- Must support swapping:
  - Optimization/broad-phase strategies
  - Physics simulation models
  - Visual shader/render styles
- Modules should be interoperable through shared interfaces.
- Small overhead for modularity is acceptable.
- Substantial overhead is not acceptable.

## Canonical runtime module contract
- Module categories:
  - `PhysicsModule`
  - `VisualModule`
  - `OptimizationModule`
- A full simulation run requires one active module from each category.
- Default visual module:
  - unlit sphere rendering (colored balls)
  - fallback behavior: if visual-module UI selection is empty/unpopulated, auto-select default visual module
  - default base color: moderate grey
  - physics/type-driven color mapping can layer on top when enabled
  - no lighting dependency required for baseline behavior

## Visual roadmap note
- Keep visual module interfaces lighting-ready, but baseline implementation stays unlit for now.
- Future goal: add optional lighting models without breaking default fallback behavior.

## Proposed modular split
- Simulation core (orchestrates frame/update pipeline)
- Broad-phase strategy module (neighbor discovery/data structure)
- Physics model module (force/constraint/integration logic)
- Visual module (particle appearance shaders/material style)
- Renderer module (draw orchestration + visualization/debug overlays)
- Experiment harness (runtime switching + metrics + comparison UI)

## Compatibility contract (draft)
- Shared particle memory layout (prefer stable GPU-friendly format).
- Shared step context (dt, bounds, constants, frame index).
- Broad-phase outputs a neighbor-query representation consumable by physics modules.
- Physics modules consume particle state + neighbor data and output updated state.
- Visual modules consume simulation/render attributes and choose shading/material behavior.
- Module dispatch decisions happen at pass/frame level (not inner per-particle branch points).

## Candidate broad-phase strategies to compare
- All-pairs naive baseline
- Uniform grid / spatial hashing
- Sort-and-sweep
- BVH variants (later phase)

## Candidate physics modules to compare
- N-body gravity
- SPH/fluid-like interactions
- Boids/flocking
- Spring-mass systems

## Demonstration goals
- Runtime method switching for side-by-side or sequential comparisons.
- Show performance and behavior differences clearly.
- Preserve reproducibility (same seed/initial state across method swaps when needed).

## Measurements to track from the start
- Frame time and simulation step time
- Neighbor-search pass time
- Physics pass time
- Particles-per-second throughput
- Memory footprint and bandwidth pressure
- Stability/energy drift metrics (module-dependent)

## Open questions
- Integrator choices and stability constraints per physics module?
- How much runtime hot-swapping is required (frame-to-frame vs restart-required)?
- Preferred UI depth for demo controls and diagnostics?

## Fixed platform targets
- Platform scope: macOS only (Apple-centric implementation is intentional).
- Naming convention for this project:
  - `M1` = Apple Silicon M1 (base), 8 GB unified memory
  - `M1.2` = Apple Silicon M1 Pro, 32 GB unified memory
- Initial hardware targets:
  - `M1.2` (M1 Pro, 32 GB)
  - `M1` (M1 base, 8 GB)
- Design intent: exploit Apple-specific capabilities where helpful (Metal/MetalKit/MPS/unified memory behavior).
- Cross-platform support is not a current objective.

## Platform expansion requirement
- VR is a future-ready target, not a current required delivery target.
- Desktop interactive mode remains the current required baseline.
- Architecture should keep simulation core independent from presentation mode so the same sim can drive:
  - desktop renderer path
  - VR renderer path
- Keep VR compatibility in design decisions so future enablement is straightforward if/when hardware is available.

## Current hardware status
- Active development/testing machine right now: `M1`.
- Near-term test passes should target `M1` constraints first.
- `M1.2` hardware is expected soon and will be added as the high-performance validation target when available.

## Immediate next planning tasks
- Define module interfaces and buffer contracts.
- Choose first broad-phase + physics pair for vertical slice.
- Define benchmark scenes and acceptance criteria.
- Create a milestone plan around throughput targets and quality constraints.

## Prior simulation reference (user design)
- Objective: produce emergent life-like structures from simple local rules.
- Particle types: `n` categories.
  - Prior interesting range: `n = 12..18` (not a hard rule).
- Interaction matrix: `M` with shape `n x n`, integer entries.
  - Prior interesting range for entries: `[-3, 3]` (not a hard rule).
  - `M` can be asymmetric (`M[a][b] != M[b][a]`), so interactions are directional by type pairing.
- Core update rule per step:
  - For each particle `i`, inspect nearby neighbors `j`.
  - For each neighbor, look up `w = M[type(i)][type(j)]`.
  - Compute unit direction from `i` to `j`: `d_ij`.
  - Add contribution `w * d_ij` to `i` velocity accumulator.
  - After summing contributions from nearby neighbors, advance particle by one timestep using resulting velocity.
- Conceptual form:
  - `v_i(t+dt) = v_i(t) + sum_over_neighbors_j( M[type_i][type_j] * d_ij )`
  - `x_i(t+dt) = x_i(t) + v_i(t+dt) * dt`
- This rule set is the core behavior to preserve as a baseline module.
- Optimizations and extra features existed previously, but are secondary to this core dynamic.

## Architecture mapping for this rule
- Physics module candidate: `TypeMatrixLocalAttractionRepulsion`.
- Broad-phase requirement: nearby-neighbor query (radius/cell-based), not all-pairs at scale.
- Data required per particle:
  - position
  - velocity
  - type index (`0..n-1`)
- Global parameters:
  - interaction matrix `M`
  - neighborhood radius
  - `dt`

## Optimization direction from prior implementation experience
- Prior implementation was highly optimized but hard to understand internally.
- New implementation goal: preserve high performance while keeping kernel structure readable and modular.
- Suspected high-value GPU optimization area:
  - threadgroup shared memory (tile/cache neighbor data per workgroup)
  - minimize repeated global memory fetches
- Design preference:
  - intuitive, inspectable kernel pipeline over "opaque cleverness"
  - clear separation between API-level configuration and compute internals

## New requirement: interactive + educational visualization
- Project must be both:
  - highly interactive
  - highly optimized
- Every optimization method should include a dedicated visualization mode that exposes its internal logic.
- Visual output should be both informative and aesthetically compelling ("cool and informative").

## Visualization ideas (method-agnostic)
- Short-lived connection bursts for active particle interactions.
- Neighborhood radius shells / influence zones.
- Cell/grid overlays for spatial partitioning methods.
- Color-coding by particle type and/or interaction sign (attract vs repel).
- Optional trails or motion vectors to show local flow.

## Visualization ideas (optimization-specific)
- All-pairs baseline mode:
  - show sampled interaction lines to communicate combinatorial growth.
- Uniform grid / spatial hash mode:
  - show occupied cells and per-particle queried neighbor cells.
- Sort-and-sweep mode:
  - show axis projection spans and overlap windows.
- BVH mode (later):
  - show hierarchy bounds and traversal paths for sampled particles.

## UX and performance constraints for visualization modes
- Visualization must be toggleable at runtime without restarting simulation.
- Provide at least two debug intensity levels:
  - sampled/low-overhead
  - dense/high-detail
- Default educational mode should use sampling/decimation to preserve interactivity.
- Keep simulation correctness independent of visualization state.
- Separate simulation timing metrics from visualization timing metrics.

## Renderer/architecture implications
- Treat debug visualization as a module layered on top of simulation outputs.
- Optimization modules should expose optional debug buffers/events (standardized schema).
- Renderer consumes these debug streams for overlays without changing physics logic.
- Support side-by-side comparisons where feasible (same initial seed, different method + debug view).

## Hardware budget policy (controlled environment)
- Deployment context is controlled/local, not broad public distribution in current phase.
- We can target known hardware characteristics directly (Apple Silicon device class and memory budget).
- System should remain configurable, but default behavior should aggressively use available hardware.
- Baseline tuning profile should target `M1.2` first.
- Near-term priority: explicit manual controls (buttons/knobs) over automatic hardware-based tuning.
- Initial memory budgets:
  - `M1.2` (M1 Pro, 32 GB): 20 GB simulation budget
  - `M1` (M1 base, 8 GB): 4 GB simulation budget

## Cross-platform ratio expectation (`M1.2` vs `M1`)
- `M1` must remain reasonably interactive and demonstrable (not just technically functional).
- `M1` particle capacity should not be disproportionately lower than memory-ratio expectations.
- Working ratio reference from initial budgets: `M1:M1.2 = 4 GB : 20 GB = 1:5`.
- Planning rule: initial `M1` particle targets should aim for roughly >= 20% of `M1.2` particle targets, subject to stability/visualization settings.

## Initial runtime presets
- `preset.m1_2.performance`
  - hardware class: `M1.2` (M1 Pro, 32 GB)
  - simulation memory budget: 20 GB
  - intent: high particle counts, aggressive optimization testing
- `preset.m1.safe`
  - hardware class: `M1` (M1 base, 8 GB)
  - simulation memory budget: 4 GB
  - intent: stable interactive baseline on constrained memory

## Budget utilization goals
- Use as much available GPU/CPU capacity as practical while maintaining interactivity.
- Auto-scale particle count and/or simulation rate toward a target utilization band.
- Keep headroom to avoid UI starvation and severe frame-time spikes.
- Particle-count ambition:
  - reliable hundreds-of-thousands baseline
  - pursue million-scale and multi-million-scale where feasible per method

## Scale milestones (draft)
- Milestone A: 100k particles interactive with debug visualization enabled (sampled mode).
- Milestone B: 500k particles interactive with optimized broad-phase.
- Milestone C: 1M particles interactive in performance mode (debug reduced/sampled).
- Stretch: >1M to multi-million depending on method and stability constraints.

## External performance benchmark to beat
- Prior reference implementation reached playable performance at ~700,000 particles on an NVIDIA RTX 2080.
- Project target: exceed that 700,000-particle playable threshold on the M1 Pro baseline if feasible.
- Benchmark comparisons should note:
  - physics rule parity (same or equivalent model)
  - visualization mode (on/off, sampled/dense)
  - frame-time definition used for "playable"

## Timing model requirement (UPS/FPS coupling)
- Use a configurable target update rate where physics updates per second (UPS) are coupled to rendered frames per second (FPS).
- Default operating mode: one physics step per rendered frame at the configured target rate.
- Target rate should be runtime-configurable (for example: 30 / 60 / 120).

## Behavior when target cannot be sustained
- If frame time exceeds budget, preserve simulation stability first.
- Prefer controlled quality scaling (debug density, then particle/detail scaling) to recover target rate.
- Avoid silent variable-`dt` drift that changes simulation character unpredictably.
- Surface real-time metrics in UI:
  - target FPS/UPS
  - actual FPS
  - actual UPS
  - current sim `dt`

## Runtime policy (draft)
- Define target budget knobs:
  - target simulation frame time
  - max memory budget for simulation + debug buffers
  - max acceptable input/render latency
- Adapt at runtime:
  - increase particle count / detail when below budget
  - reduce debug density / sampling first when over budget
  - reduce particle count or sim frequency only after debug reductions

## Runtime controls priority
- Phase 1 (high priority): build manual runtime controls first.
  - particle count
  - simulation target rate (FPS/UPS)
  - debug visualization intensity/mode
  - neighborhood radius / key physics parameters
- Phase 2 (lower priority): add hardware detection and optional auto-configuration.
  - detect unified memory/GPU tier/CPU availability
  - propose defaults or auto-tune ranges based on detected hardware

## Measurement additions for budget-aware operation
- Hardware utilization estimates (GPU busy, CPU busy proxy metrics where available).
- Memory pressure against configured simulation budget.
- Budget controller actions over time (what was scaled and when).
